.TH OZFS-IMPORT 1ONBLD "November 15, 2026"
.SH NAME
 ozfs-import \- prepare or apply OpenZFS changes with illumos paths
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBozfs-import\fR [\fB--config\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB--source\fR \fIrepository\fR]
    [\fB--destination\fR \fIrepository\fR] [\fB--import-paths\fR \fIfile\fR]
    [\fB--group\fR \fIlabel\fR] [\fB--output\fR \fIfile\fR] \fIcommit\fR ...

\fBozfs-import\fR [\fIoptions\fR] \fB--check\fR \fIcommit\fR ...
\fBozfs-import\fR [\fIoptions\fR] \fB--apply\fR \fIpatch-file\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B ozfs-import
command converts an ordered list of OpenZFS commits into one mail patch
whose paths correspond to an illumos Git worktree.
The list represents one complete logical change; the command does not
select prerequisites or discover follow-up fixes.
Merge commits are not supported.
.LP
By default, the command writes the patch in the current directory as
.BR 0001-subject.patch ,
using Git's filename-safe form of the first source commit's subject.
The filename is limited to 64 characters, with a fallback subject of
.B patch
when the sanitized subject is empty.
Use
.B --output
to choose another filename.
The command also writes a sibling file with the suffix
.B .manifest.tsv
that records source commits and path classifications.
Neither file may already exist.
The patch preserves the first source commit's subject, author, and author
date, and includes each selected commit's full hash and original message.
Applying it produces one illumos commit.
.LP
Below the commit-message separator, the patch includes a Git diffstat and
file-operation summary.
For a combined work item, the summary describes the ordered source diffs,
so a file changed by several commits may appear more than once.
If paths require review, a
.B Requires review
section follows the summary and lists their source paths, mapped destinations
when known, and reasons.
These annotations travel with the patch but are excluded from the commit
message by
.BR "git am" .
.LP
Configured mappings rewrite file paths, with the longest source prefix
taking precedence.
A unique existing destination can resolve multiple possible mappings;
otherwise the mapping requires review.
Configured exclusions and unselected platform variants are omitted and
reported in the manifest.
Unclassified paths, exceptional native implementations, ambiguous mappings,
missing targets, and unapproved additions require review.
Only the
.B [additions]
section authorizes new destination paths.
The shipped policy is a starting point and must be reviewed for the
particular source and destination revisions.
.LP
Preparation writes only the mail patch and manifest.
It changes neither the destination object database, index, worktree, nor branch.
The source OpenZFS repository is read through Git objects and is not changed.
A prepared patch can subsequently be applied with:
.LP
.nf
    git am --3way change.mbox
.fi
.LP
Manual application can use three-way fallback only when the preimage blobs
are already available in that destination.
To copy the required blobs and apply the prepared patch in one operation,
use
.B --apply
with its filename.
.LP
If review is required, preparation still writes the complete patch and
manifest, then returns nonzero.
Unresolved paths retain their OpenZFS names so the changes remain visible.
Such a patch is review material and may require editing before application.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--config " file
Read local settings from the specified INI file.
No local configuration file is read implicitly.
Relative paths in this file are resolved from its containing directory.
.TP
.BI "--source " repository
Select the OpenZFS Git worktree.
Overrides the configuration file's source setting.
A source must be specified by option or configuration.
.TP
.BI "--destination " repository
Select the illumos Git worktree.
Overrides the configuration file's destination setting.
The default is the current Git worktree.
Relative paths supplied as options are resolved from the current directory.
.TP
.BI "--import-paths " file
Use a replacement INI path policy instead of the shipped
.B import-paths.conf
file.
Overrides the configuration file's
.B paths.import-paths
setting.
.TP
.BI "--output " file
Write a new mail patch and manifest.
Overrides the default
.B 0001-subject.patch
filename.
Incompatible with
.B --check
and
.BR --apply .
.TP
.B --check
Report commit and path classifications without writing artifacts, copying
objects, or invoking
.BR "git am" .
Incompatible with
.BR --apply .
.TP
.B --apply
Read one supplied mail patch and extract full
.B OpenZFS
commit hashes from its commit message.
Locate the required preimage blobs in the parents of those source commits,
copy and verify them in the destination Git object database, and invoke
.B git am --3way
once with the supplied patch.
Existing native preimage blobs may also be used.
The patch is applied as supplied, preserving review edits to its message
and diff.
No source commits need to be supplied as arguments.
A source repository must still be specified by configuration or
.BR --source .
The operation accepts exactly one mail message, which may contain a
combined logical change from several source commits.
It does not read mapping files or the preparation manifest.
The
.B --group
and
.B --import-paths
options apply only during preparation and checking.
Requires a clean tracked worktree and index and no pending
.B git am
operation or retained import artifacts.
.TP
.BI "--group " label
Add an optional
.B Import
label to the generated commit message.
The label is a nonempty single line; no research group numbering is required.
.SH CONFIGURATION
For example:
.LP
.nf
    [repositories]
    openzfs = ../openzfs
    illumos = .

    [paths]
    import-paths = ./my-import-paths.conf
.fi
.LP
All settings are optional if their values are supplied as options or defaults.
Shipped path policy resides in
.BR /opt/onbld/etc/ozfs/import-paths.conf .
When run from source, the command uses
.BR $SRC/tools/ozfs/import-paths.conf .
.SH PATH POLICY
The policy uses INI syntax, parsed with Python's standard-library
.BR configparser .
Paths are case sensitive and relative to their respective Git tree roots.
Comments start with
.B #
or
.B ;
on separate lines.
Inline comments and variable interpolation are disabled.
The only key/value delimiter is
.BR = .
Unknown sections, duplicate keys, and malformed entries are errors.
The
.B DEFAULT
section must not contain values.
Omitted sections are treated as empty.
.LP
For example:
.LP
.nf
    [map]
    module/zfs = usr/src/uts/common/fs/zfs
    include/sys = usr/src/common/zfs
        usr/src/uts/common/fs/zfs/sys
    include/sys/nvpair.h = usr/src/uts/common/sys/nvpair.h

    [additions]
    tests/zfs-tests = usr/src/test/zfs-tests

    [exclude-openzfs]
    lib/libshare
    :(glob)**/Makefile.am

    [review-illumos]
    usr/src/uts/common/fs/zfs/zfs_acl.c

    [primary-freebsd]
    module/os/freebsd/zfs/arc_os.c = usr/src/uts/common/fs/zfs/arc.c

    [alternate-linux]
    module/os/linux/zfs/arc_os.c = usr/src/uts/common/fs/zfs/arc.c
.fi
.LP
The supported sections are:
.TP
.B [map]
Entries map an OpenZFS file or directory prefix to an illumos counterpart.
Relative paths beneath a mapped directory are preserved.
Indented continuation lines list alternative destinations for one source.
The longest source prefix wins; among equally specific destinations, a
unique existing counterpart resolves the mapping.
Remaining ambiguity requires review.
ZFS-private headers normally belong under
.BR uts/common/fs/zfs/sys ;
native platform headers use explicit filename mappings into
.BR uts/common/sys .
Headers shared with userland may belong under
.BR common/zfs .
.TP
.B [additions]
Source-to-destination mappings that also permit new destination paths.
These take precedence over ordinary mappings.
.TP
.BR [exclude-openzfs] ", " [exclude-illumos]
Paths without values.
An excluded OpenZFS path is omitted and reported.
Mapping into an excluded illumos path is an error.
Literal paths select a file or a directory subtree.
The
.B :(glob)
prefix selects a glob pattern.
.TP
.BR [review-openzfs] ", " [review-illumos]
Paths without values, using the same literal and glob syntax.
These select exceptional or intentionally divergent code for review.
Their diffs remain visible in prepared review artifacts.
.TP
.BR [primary-freebsd] ", " [primary-linux]
Map explicitly selected platform-specific source files or directories to
their illumos counterparts.
Each illumos destination has at most one primary.
.TP
.BR [alternate-freebsd] ", " [alternate-linux]
Map alternate platform source paths to the corresponding illumos destination.
Changes to alternates are omitted and reported.
Each alternate must have a primary for its destination.
No platform source path may appear in more than one platform section.
.LP
The policy is read only during preparation and checking.
Applying a reviewed patch uses the paths already present in that patch.
.SH CONFLICT HANDLING
With
.BR --apply ,
an operation manifest is stored at the destination Git path
.BR ozfs-import.manifest.tsv .
Use
.B git rev-parse --git-path
with this name to locate it, including in linked worktrees.
It records source hashes, the starting illumos commit, and a digest of the
supplied patch.
Successful application removes the operation manifest.
Failed application retains it for inspection.
.LP
When Git stops with a conflict, the command returns nonzero and preserves
Git's operation state.
Resolve the files, stage the resolutions, and run
.BR "git am --continue" .
Alternatively, deliberately use
.BR "git am --abort" ,
.BR "git am --skip" ,
or
.BR "git am --quit" .
The command never performs these recovery operations itself.
After settling the operation, remove the retained import manifest before
starting another invocation with
.BR --apply .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested check, preparation, or application completed without a review
stop, or all changed paths were omitted by policy and no patch was needed.
.TP
.B 1
An error, required path review, or failed application prevented completion.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line syntax.
.SH EXAMPLES
Prepare one selected change for manual review and import:
.LP
.nf
    ozfs-import --source ../openzfs --output change.mbox COMMIT
    git am --3way change.mbox
.fi
.LP
Prepare a selected change and its follow-up, then apply the combined patch:
.LP
.nf
    ozfs-import --source ../openzfs --output combined.patch COMMIT FOLLOWUP
    ozfs-import --source ../openzfs --apply combined.patch
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-am (1),
.BR git-apply (1)
